<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indica</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Indika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indica is an account of Mauryan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by the Greek writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megasthenes">Megasthenes</a>, who died around 290 BCE. The original is lost, but fragments survive in later Greek and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> works, the earliest by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diodorus-siculus">Diodorus Siculus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a>, Pliny and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arrian">Arrian</a>; E. A. Schwanbeck collected them in 1846, and John Watson McCrindle published a reconstruction in 1877 that is not universally accepted. It described India's land, peoples and customs, and divided its society into seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, where Indian texts name four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">varnas</a>. Strabo called Megasthenes a liar for his tales of mouthless tribes and gold-digging ants, but some authors consider the work an important source on the society and administration of its time.</p></div>
